<Layout pageTitle="Peripheral nerve lab guide" subtitle="Controls, experiments, and interpretation."><article><p><a href="/simulations/nerve-cross-section/">Open the lab →</a></p><h2>Start with one variable</h2><ol><li>Choose Focused contact. The same 1,200 sites are used each time.</li><li>Drag the contact within F1, or type X/Y coordinates.</li><li>Increase amplitude and compare target recruitment with recruitment of other fascicles.</li><li>Click another fascicle to change the target. Compare Cuff pair and Four contacts presets.</li><li>Save JSON to retain the exact experiment. Download sweep CSV to analyze the 0–150 a.u. amplitude sweep.</li></ol><h2>What the numbers mean</h2><p>Target recruitment is the percentage of sites recruited in the selected fascicle. Other recruitment is the percentage in the other three fascicles combined. Target share is the fraction of all recruited sites belonging to the target; it is undefined when no sites recruit.</p><h2>Model</h2><p>Each contact contributes A × exp(−distance / spread). All contributions have the same sign. Each sample site's fixed threshold is 0.8–1.2 times the global threshold. The nerve diameter is 2 mm; spatial units are mm, and amplitude/threshold are arbitrary units. The model does not predict clinical stimulation or model pulse timing. The cuff preset is a geometric comparison, not a solved bipolar field.</p></article></Layout>
